};

const schemaJson = JSON.stringify(schemaData);

const sampleNegativeCovenantClause = `# Standard Corporate Protection Clause: Limitation of Contracting Authority
1. NO AUTHORITY TO BIND:
   The Contractor / EOR Assigned Worker expressly acknowledges and agrees that they do
   not possess, and shall not represent themselves as possessing, any legal, commercial,
   or apparent authority to negotiate, amend, conclude, or execute any binding contract,
   undertaking, or agreement on behalf of the Principal Enterprise.

2. CONTRACT EXECUTION RESTRICTION:
   All customer proposals, master service agreements (MSAs), statements of work (SOWs),
   and pricing commitments must be submitted to the Principal's executive headquarters
   and executed solely by authorized corporate officers residing outside the Contractor's
   local jurisdiction.

3. MARKETING & REPRESENTATION:
   The Contractor / EOR Worker shall not utilize corporate executive titles (e.g.,
   "Managing Director", "Vice President", "General Manager") on email signatures,
   business cards, or public professional profiles (LinkedIn) without the qualifying
   suffix "(External Consultant)" or "(via Employer of Record)".`;
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical} schemaJson={schemaJson} type="article">
  <article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto pt-4 pb-16">
    <!-- Breadcrumb -->
    <nav class="flex items-center space-x-2 text-xs font-mono text-slate-400 mb-6" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-emerald-400 transition">EORCalculator</a>
      <span>/</span>
      <a href="/contractor-vs-eor-legal-misclassification-risk-matrix/" class="hover:text-emerald-400 transition">Compliance</a>
      <span>/</span>
      <span class="text-emerald-400">Permanent Establishment Risk</span>
    </nav>

    <!-- Exact 1 H1 -->
    <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl md:text-5xl font-black tracking-tight text-white leading-tight mb-6">
      B2B Contractor vs EOR: Avoiding Permanent Establishment (PE) Tax Triggers
    </h1>

    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 text-xs text-slate-400 font-mono mb-8">
      <span class="px-2.5 py-1 rounded bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800 text-slate-300">Framework: OECD Model Tax Convention Art. 5</span>
      <span class="px-2.5 py-1 rounded bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800 text-emerald-400">Target: CFOs, General Counsels &amp; Global People Ops</span>
      <span>Updated: September 2026</span>
    </div>

    <!-- 45-60 Word Quick Answer Box with green accent border -->
    <div class="bg-gradient-to-br from-slate-900 via-slate-900/90 to-slate-950 border-2 border-emerald-500 rounded-2xl p-6 shadow-xl shadow-emerald-500/10 mb-10">
      <div class="flex items-center space-x-2.5 text-xs font-bold font-mono uppercase tracking-wider text-emerald-400 mb-2">
        <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-emerald-400" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
          <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M12 9v2m0 4h.01m-6.938 4h13.856c1.54 0 2.502-1.667 1.732-3L13.732 4c-.77-1.333-2.694-1.333-3.464 0L3.34 16c-.77 1.333.192 3 1.732 3z" />
        </svg>
        <span>Quick Answer: Permanent Establishment (PE) Tax Triggers</span>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm md:text-base text-slate-200 leading-relaxed font-medium">
        Hiring international remote staff via direct B2B contractor contracts risks triggering Permanent Establishment (PE) under OECD Article 5 if workers negotiate commercial deals, hold executive titles, or work exclusively for your firm. An Employer of Record (EOR) mitigates PE risk by legally employing the worker through a compliant local entity and handling statutory tax withholding.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Main Content -->
    <div class="prose prose-invert max-w-none space-y-10 text-slate-300 text-sm sm:text-base leading-relaxed">
      
      <!-- Section 1: The Permanent Establishment Threat -->
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white tracking-tight mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="text-emerald-400 font-mono">1.</span> The Corporate Tax Threat: What is Permanent Establishment?
        </h2>
        <p>
          When hiring remote talent in international jurisdictions—whether software engineers in Poland, sales executives in the United Kingdom, or account managers in Brazil—most corporate executives focus exclusively on worker misclassification risks. They calculate the threat in terms of backdated payroll taxes, unpaid overtime, statutory leave entitlements, and severance penalties.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4">
          However, experienced Chief Financial Officers and international tax attorneys recognize that worker misclassification is only a localized employment dispute. A far more devastating corporate financial liability is <strong>Permanent Establishment (PE)</strong>.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4">
          Under bilateral tax treaties governed by the <strong>OECD Model Tax Convention</strong>, if a national tax authority (such as the UK's HMRC, Germany's Bundeszentralamt für Steuern, or France's Direction Générale des Finances Publiques) determines that a foreign corporation maintains an ongoing taxable presence through local personnel, it pierces the foreign entity veil and exercises corporate tax jurisdiction.
        </p>
        <div class="bg-slate-900/80 border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-5 my-6">
          <div class="text-xs font-mono text-rose-400 uppercase font-bold mb-2">Consequences of a Retroactive PE Assessment:</div>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-200">
            <li><strong>Corporate Income Tax on Attributed Global Profits:</strong> The host nation levies statutory corporate income tax (typically 20% to 34%) on the share of your global revenue deemed attributable to the in-country operations, backdated up to 5 to 7 years.</li>
            <li><strong>Catastrophic Double Taxation:</strong> If your home country revenue authority does not recognize the foreign tax assessment under foreign tax credit rules, corporate earnings are taxed twice without treaty relief.</li>
            <li><strong>Mandatory Transfer Pricing Audits:</strong> Tax examiners apply the Authorized OECD Approach (AOA) to reconstruct intercompany transactions, forcing punitive cost-plus margins.</li>
            <li><strong>Severe Criminal &amp; Civil Penalties:</strong> Failure to register a taxable corporate presence triggers compounding interest, late-filing penalties, and personal liability risks for corporate officers.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 2: Legal Risk Matrix Under OECD Article 5 -->
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white tracking-tight mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="text-emerald-400 font-mono">2.</span> Legal Risk Matrix: OECD Model Tax Convention Article 5
        </h2>
        <p>
          The OECD Model Tax Convention establishes clear legal mechanisms through which an international worker or independent contractor triggers corporate tax nexus for an overseas parent enterprise:
        </p>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-slate-800 bg-slate-950 my-6 shadow-xl">
          <table class="w-full text-left text-xs text-slate-300">
            <thead class="bg-slate-900 text-slate-200 font-mono uppercase text-[11px] border-b border-slate-800">
              <tr>
                <th class="py-3 px-4">PE Trigger Category</th>
                <th class="py-3 px-4">OECD Legal Basis</th>
                <th class="py-3 px-4">Factual Indicators &amp; Operational Triggers</th>
                <th class="py-3 px-4">Risk Level with Contractors</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-800 font-mono">
              <tr>
                <td class="py-3 px-4 font-bold text-rose-400">Dependent Agent PE</td>
                <td class="py-3 px-4 text-white">Article 5(5) &amp; 5(6)</td>
                <td class="py-3 px-4 font-sans text-slate-300">Worker habitually negotiates pricing, signs sales agreements, or leads commercial deal closings without substantive corporate review.</td>
                <td class="py-3 px-4 text-rose-400 font-bold">EXTREME (Critical)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="py-3 px-4 font-bold text-amber-400">Fixed Place of Business PE</td>
                <td class="py-3 px-4 text-white">Article 5(1)</td>
                <td class="py-3 px-4 font-sans text-slate-300">Home office is "at the disposal of the enterprise" because the foreign company mandates remote presence, pays rent stipends, or holds client meetings there.</td>
                <td class="py-3 px-4 text-amber-400 font-bold">HIGH</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="py-3 px-4 font-bold text-yellow-400">Service PE</td>
                <td class="py-3 px-4 text-white">UN Model / Bilateral Treaties</td>
                <td class="py-3 px-4 font-sans text-slate-300">Providing technical consulting, software implementation, or engineering services in-country exceeding 183 days in a 12-month period.</td>
                <td class="py-3 px-4 text-yellow-400 font-bold">MODERATE - HIGH</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          The 2017 OECD Base Erosion and Profit Shifting (BEPS) Action 7 update significantly broadened the definition of a Dependent Agent. Previously, companies avoided PE if the foreign contractor did not formally sign the contract. Under current BEPS rules, if a contractor plays the <em>principal role leading to the conclusion of contracts</em> that are routinely approved by headquarters without material modification, a Dependent Agent PE is deemed to exist.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 3: Fixed Place of Business PE Under Article 5(1) -->
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white tracking-tight mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="text-emerald-400 font-mono">3.</span> Fixed Place of Business PE Under Article 5(1): Does a Home Office Create Nexus?
        </h2>
        <p>
          Can an employee or contractor working from their spare bedroom create a multi-million-dollar corporate tax liability? Under OECD Article 5(1), a Permanent Establishment exists if there is a "fixed place of business through which the business of an enterprise is wholly or partly carried on."
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4">
          According to OECD Commentary on Article 5, a home office creates a Fixed Place of Business PE when it is <strong>at the disposal of the foreign enterprise</strong>. Tax authorities assess three factual conditions:
        </p>

        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-4 my-6">
          <div class="bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-5">
            <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-white mb-2">1. The Necessity Test</h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
              Does the enterprise require the individual to work from home because no commercial office space is provided in that country, while the nature of the business requires a continuous physical presence?
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-5">
            <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-white mb-2">2. Financial Contribution</h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
              Does the company reimburse residential rent, utility expenses, or dedicated high-speed internet lines specifically tied to maintaining a permanent foreign operational base?
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-5">
            <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-white mb-2">3. Commercial Representation</h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
              Does the enterprise advertise the worker's home address on its corporate website, business cards, or local supplier directories as its localized regional contact center?
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 4: Comprehensive Comparison Matrix: Direct Contractor vs EOR -->
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white tracking-tight mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="text-emerald-400 font-mono">4.</span> Comprehensive Comparison Matrix: Direct B2B Contractor vs. Employer of Record (EOR)
        </h2>
        <p>
          To understand how an Employer of Record (EOR) changes your risk profile, examine how local tax and labor authorities evaluate both engagement structures across key operational dimensions:
        </p>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-slate-800 bg-slate-950 my-6 shadow-xl">
          <table class="w-full text-left text-xs text-slate-300">
            <thead class="bg-slate-900 text-slate-200 font-mono uppercase text-[11px] border-b border-slate-800">
              <tr>
                <th class="py-3 px-4">Compliance Dimension</th>
                <th class="py-3 px-4">Direct B2B Contractor Engagement</th>
                <th class="py-3 px-4">Employer of Record (EOR) Structure</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-800 font-mono">
              <tr>
                <td class="py-3 px-4 font-bold text-white">Legal Employer of Record</td>
                <td class="py-3 px-4 text-rose-400">None (Deemed direct employment by parent)</td>
                <td class="py-3 px-4 text-emerald-400 font-bold">Local registered EOR subsidiary</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="py-3 px-4 font-bold text-white">Corporate Entity Boundary</td>
                <td class="py-3 px-4 text-rose-400">Zero corporate insulation; judgments attach to parent</td>
                <td class="py-3 px-4 text-emerald-400 font-bold">Fully interposed corporate legal entity</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="py-3 px-4 font-bold text-white">Payroll Tax &amp; Social Withholding</td>
                <td class="py-3 px-4 text-rose-400">Transferred to contractor (often under-reported)</td>
                <td class="py-3 px-4 text-emerald-400 font-bold">100% compliant local statutory withholding</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="py-3 px-4 font-bold text-white">Dependent Agent PE Defense</td>
                <td class="py-3 px-4 text-rose-400">Extremely fragile; personal actions bind parent</td>
                <td class="py-3 px-4 text-emerald-400 font-bold">Strong defense if sales authority restricted</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="py-3 px-4 font-bold text-white">Audit Exposure Route</td>
                <td class="py-3 px-4 text-rose-400">Direct cross-border tax audit on parent firm</td>
                <td class="py-3 px-4 text-emerald-400 font-bold">Localized audit managed by EOR entity</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="py-3 px-4 font-bold text-white">Intellectual Property Assignment</td>
                <td class="py-3 px-4 text-amber-400">Subject to local moral rights &amp; assignment voids</td>
                <td class="py-3 px-4 text-emerald-400 font-bold">Back-to-back statutory IP transfer agreement</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 5: 8-Point Corporate Tax Risk Checklist -->
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white tracking-tight mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="text-emerald-400 font-mono">5.</span> 8-Point Corporate Tax Risk Checklist: Assessing Contractor vs. EOR PE Vulnerabilities
        </h2>
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-400 mb-6">
          Evaluate your global remote team against these eight operational criteria to determine whether your current workforce structure will trigger corporate tax assessments during a tax examination:
        </p>

        <div class="space-y-4">
          <!-- Item 1 -->
          <div class="bg-slate-900/70 border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-5">
            <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-2">
              <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-white">1. Commercial Contract Execution Authority</h3>
              <span class="text-xs font-mono text-rose-400 bg-rose-950/60 px-2 py-0.5 rounded border border-rose-900">Highest PE Vector</span>
            </div>
            <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-2">
              <strong>Direct Contractor:</strong> If an international sales representative signs contracts or negotiates binding commercial terms, local tax authorities classify them as an authorized agent under OECD Article 5(5), triggering immediate corporate income tax exposure on all associated regional sales.
            </p>
            <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-emerald-300 leading-relaxed">
              <strong>EOR Shielding:</strong> EOR employment agreements expressly specify that the worker cannot bind either the EOR or the client enterprise commercially. All enterprise master service agreements must be executed directly by headquarters corporate officers outside the jurisdiction.
            </p>
          </div>

          <!-- Item 2 -->
          <div class="bg-slate-900/70 border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-5">
            <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-2">
              <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-white">2. Core Business Activity vs. Auxiliary Functions</h3>
              <span class="text-xs font-mono text-amber-400 bg-amber-950/60 px-2 py-0.5 rounded border border-amber-900">OECD Art. 5(4)</span>
            </div>
            <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-2">
              <strong>Direct Contractor:</strong> Preparatory or auxiliary activities (such as localized language translation or market data gathering) are exempt from PE under Article 5(4). However, core revenue-generating product development or direct customer onboarding conducted by contractors breaks this safe harbor.
            </p>
            <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-emerald-300 leading-relaxed">
              <strong>EOR Shielding:</strong> An EOR's in-country subsidiary files local corporate returns and pays employer payroll contributions, providing a documented administrative buffer for core engineering teams.
            </p>
          </div>

          <!-- Item 3 -->
          <div class="bg-slate-900/70 border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-5">
            <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-2">
              <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-white">3. Economic Exclusivity &amp; Single-Client Dependence</h3>
              <span class="text-xs font-mono text-amber-400 bg-amber-950/60 px-2 py-0.5 rounded border border-amber-900">Dependency Test</span>
            </div>
            <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-2">
              <strong>Direct Contractor:</strong> When 100% of an independent contractor's annual invoicing originates from your company, tax courts classify them as an economically dependent agent rather than an independent market participant.
            </p>
            <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-emerald-300 leading-relaxed">
              <strong>EOR Shielding:</strong> EOR workers are full statutory employees of the EOR entity; economic exclusivity is legally expected, standard, and fully compliant under national labor codes.
            </p>
          </div>

          <!-- Item 4 -->
          <div class="bg-slate-900/70 border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-5">
            <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-2">
              <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-white">4. Managerial Subordination &amp; Direct Supervision</h3>
              <span class="text-xs font-mono text-yellow-400 bg-yellow-950/60 px-2 py-0.5 rounded border border-yellow-900">Substance Over Form</span>
            </div>
            <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-2">
              <strong>Direct Contractor:</strong> Daily standups, performance appraisals, fixed working hours, and time-tracking tools serve as definitive proof of employment and corporate integration during tax audits.
            </p>
            <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-emerald-300 leading-relaxed">
              <strong>EOR Shielding:</strong> The EOR structure legally authorizes operational supervision via a tripartite co-employment or service delivery agreement while satisfying statutory employer duties.
            </p>
          </div>

          <!-- Item 5 -->
          <div class="bg-slate-900/70 border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-5">
            <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-2">
              <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-white">5. Dedicated Home Office Equipment &amp; Disposal</h3>
              <span class="text-xs font-mono text-yellow-400 bg-yellow-950/60 px-2 py-0.5 rounded border border-yellow-900">Fixed Place Test</span>
            </div>
            <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-2">
              <strong>Direct Contractor:</strong> Providing branded laptops, paying dedicated coworking stipends, or paying home office allowances can convert the domestic premises into a "place at the disposal of the principal".
            </p>
            <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-emerald-300 leading-relaxed">
              <strong>EOR Shielding:</strong> Equipment allowances and expense reimbursements flow through the local EOR payroll and are taxed under local fringe benefit regulations.
            </p>
          </div>

          <!-- Item 6 -->
          <div class="bg-slate-900/70 border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-5">
            <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-2">
              <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-white">6. Public Representation &amp; Executive Titles</h3>
              <span class="text-xs font-mono text-rose-400 bg-rose-950/60 px-2 py-0.5 rounded border border-rose-900">Apparent Authority</span>
            </div>
            <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-2">
              <strong>Direct Contractor:</strong> Giving a contractor corporate email addresses (@company.com) without disclaimer suffixes, or permitting titles like "Managing Director, EMEA" on LinkedIn, creates apparent authority that auditors cite during inspections.
            </p>
            <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-emerald-300 leading-relaxed">
              <strong>EOR Shielding:</strong> Contracts delineate operational functional titles from legal representative powers, restricting apparent authority.
            </p>
          </div>

          <!-- Item 7 -->
          <div class="bg-slate-900/70 border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-5">
            <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-2">
              <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-white">7. Local Payroll &amp; Employer Withholding Compliance</h3>
              <span class="text-xs font-mono text-emerald-400 bg-emerald-950/60 px-2 py-0.5 rounded border border-emerald-900">Tax Reporting</span>
            </div>
            <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-2">
              <strong>Direct Contractor:</strong> Tax authorities cross-reference international wire transfers with contractor tax filings. Discrepancies prompt corporate investigations into whether the foreign payer owes back employer taxes.
            </p>
            <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-emerald-300 leading-relaxed">
              <strong>EOR Shielding:</strong> The EOR executes monthly statutory filings, withholds income tax at source, and remits compulsory social security contributions directly to the national treasury.
            </p>
          </div>

          <!-- Item 8 -->
          <div class="bg-slate-900/70 border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-5">
            <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-2">
              <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-white">8. Corporate Entity Shielding &amp; Liability Interposition</h3>
              <span class="text-xs font-mono text-emerald-400 bg-emerald-950/60 px-2 py-0.5 rounded border border-emerald-900">Entity Boundary</span>
            </div>
            <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-2">
              <strong>Direct Contractor:</strong> Zero corporate separation exists between your foreign headquarters and the local tax auditor. Any judgment attaches directly to your parent corporation.
            </p>
            <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-emerald-300 leading-relaxed">
              <strong>EOR Shielding:</strong> The EOR's in-country legal entity acts as the statutory employer of record, absorbing employer liability, managing workers' compensation, and handling local employment disputes.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 6: When Even an EOR Cannot Protect You -->
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white tracking-tight mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="text-emerald-400 font-mono">6.</span> Critical Warning: When Even an Employer of Record Cannot Protect Against PE
        </h2>
        <div class="bg-rose-950/30 border border-rose-900/60 rounded-xl p-6">
          <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-rose-400 mb-2">The Limits of EOR Corporate Tax Immunity</h3>
          <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-rose-200 leading-relaxed">
            <strong>Critical Legal Distinction:</strong> An Employer of Record (EOR) solves labor law compliance, employment contract administration, and statutory payroll tax withholdings. However, <strong>an EOR is not a corporate tax shield for commercial activities</strong>.
          </p>
          <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-rose-200 leading-relaxed mt-3">
            If an employee hired through an EOR in Frankfurt or London habitually negotiates enterprise sales contracts, agrees on discounting schedules, and signs customer agreements on behalf of your US Delaware corporation, local revenue authorities will disregard the EOR entity and declare a <strong>Dependent Agent Permanent Establishment</strong> directly on the US parent entity.
          </p>
          <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-rose-200 leading-relaxed mt-3">
            To prevent this, companies employing international revenue teams via EOR must establish strict operational governance: all contract negotiations must be characterized as non-binding preliminary discussions, and final legal approval and DocuSign execution must occur exclusively by officers located at corporate headquarters.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 7: Profit Attribution and Transfer Pricing Under AOA -->
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white tracking-tight mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="text-emerald-400 font-mono">7.</span> Profit Attribution and Transfer Pricing Under the Authorized OECD Approach (AOA)
        </h2>
        <p>
          If a national tax agency successfully asserts that your company has triggered a Permanent Establishment, how do they determine how much tax you owe? They apply the <strong>Authorized OECD Approach (AOA)</strong> codified in Article 7 of the Model Tax Convention.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4">
          Under the AOA, the Permanent Establishment is treated as a separate, independent enterprise performing similar functions under similar conditions. Tax auditors execute a <strong>Functional and Factual Analysis (FAR)</strong>:
        </p>

        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-4 my-6">
          <div class="bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-5">
            <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-emerald-400 mb-2">Functions Performed</h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
              Evaluating the economic importance of the activities carried out locally (e.g. core software algorithm development vs minor bug fixes; enterprise sales closing vs lead qualification).
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-5">
            <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-cyan-400 mb-2">Assets Utilized</h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
              Determining whether valuable intangible property (IP, source code, patented designs, customer lists) was developed or managed by the local personnel.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-slate-900/60 border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-5">
            <h3 class="text-sm font-bold text-amber-400 mb-2">Risks Assumed</h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
              Assessing whether local workers bear product warranty risks, commercial credit default risks, or operational liability on behalf of the corporate group.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          Based on the FAR analysis, the tax authority applies transfer pricing methodologies (typically the Cost Plus Method or Transactional Net Margin Method / TNMM). They determine an arm's-length profit markup (often cost plus 8% to 15%), treat this deemed profit as taxable corporate income in the country, and levy corporate income tax plus retroactive non-filing penalties.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 8: Operational Governance Protocol -->
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white tracking-tight mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="text-emerald-400 font-mono">8.</span> Operational Governance Protocol: Drafting Safe Contractor Agreements and EOR Work Orders
        </h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          To protect your business against catastrophic PE liabilities, incorporate explicit negative covenants into all international contractor agreements and EOR statement-of-work addenda:
        </p>

        <div class="bg-slate-950 border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-5 mb-6">
          <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-3">
            <span class="text-xs font-mono text-slate-400 font-bold uppercase">Corporate Protection Clause:</span>
            <span class="text-[11px] font-mono text-emerald-400">Legal Specimen</span>
          </div>
          <pre class="bg-slate-900 text-slate-200 p-4 rounded-lg font-mono text-xs overflow-x-auto border border-slate-800 leading-relaxed" is:raw>{sampleNegativeCovenantClause}</pre>
        </div>

        <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
          Additionally, conduct an annual internal workforce audit. When an international contractor reaches 12 continuous months of tenure, works exclusively for your company, or transitions into a managerial position directing other staff, immediately transition them to an Employer of Record or assist them in formalizing an independent corporate entity in their home jurisdiction.
        </p>
      </section>

